## Complexity Analysis

Let `n` be the size of `nums`.

### Time Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

* Finding the consecutive prefix: `O(n)`
* Building the hash set: `O(n)`
* Searching for the missing integer: `O(n)` in the worst case

Overall:

```text
O(n)
```

### Space Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

The `unordered_set` stores all elements of the array.

---

## C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int missingInteger(vector<int>& nums) {

        // Sum of the longest consecutive prefix
        int sum = nums[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1] + 1) {
                sum += nums[i];
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }

        // Store all elements for O(1) average lookup
        unordered_set<int> seen(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        // Find the smallest integer >= sum
        // that is not present in nums
        int answer = sum;

        while (seen.count(answer)) {
            answer++;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

## Key Insight

The problem can be separated into two independent tasks:

```text
Longest consecutive prefix
          ↓
       Calculate sum
          ↓
Check whether sum exists
          ↓
Increment until missing
          ↓
       Final answer
```

The `unordered_set` makes checking whether a candidate integer exists in the array efficient.
